gm-method debugging lesson (shared-depth-buffer compositing): when consumer geometry 'draws over' a backdrop renderer's terrain that shares the canvas depth buffer, and ALL consumer materials read correct (depthWrite/depthTest true, opaque) via live material readback, the cause is usually that the backdrop renders into its OWN offscreen FBO (depth-renderbuffer) under some condition (e.g. a half-res water/effect pass active at ground level), so its depth lands in the FBO not the shared canvas depth buffer -> consumer has nothing to depth-test against. Eliminate the material hypotheses FIRST by reading material.depthWrite/depthTest/transparent live on the page (cheap, decisive), then read the backdrop's render-target path. The fix is often a single consumer-set flag forcing direct-to-canvas rendering. ALSO: AskUserQuestion to pin the exact visual symptom ('over distant terrain' vs 'over entities' vs 'flat billboard band') when live screenshots can't reproduce the reported artifact - it redirects the search from a non-bug to the real one. TOOLING: the managed browser daemon crashes (UV_HANDLE_CLOSING / session-not-found) on scripts with long in-page waits (multi-second setTimeout); use a self-contained direct-playwright launcher (own chromium, long timeout) for those, and keep the spool browser verb for SHORT capture-prefixed reads that mark the witness hashes. The browser verb body is raw JS after a bare `capture` line - NEVER put filenames on the capture line (they eval as code).